Start with a working conversation: one program publishes an object, and
another calls it over encrypted TCP. The Zig example runs both peers for you.
If you prefer another language, choose its guide:

> []($block.attrs('link-grid'))
> - [Zig]($link.ref('run-a-complete-example'))
> - [C](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md)
> - [Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust'))
> - [TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts'))
> - [Python]($link.page('bindings/python'))
> - [Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket'))
> - [In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser'))

# [Run a complete example]($section.id('run-a-complete-example'))

Clone the project and install its pinned Zig compiler under your home
directory. On Linux or macOS you need Git, Python 3, Bash, curl, tar, and xz
support, and the installer verifies its download. On Windows, enable Developer Mode and
configure Git to preserve symlinks first, as
[Get a working checkout](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#get-a-working-checkout)
explains.

```sh
git clone https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap.git
cd glowcap
ZIG_INSTALL_DIR="$HOME/.local/zig" bash .devcontainer/image/zig.sh
export PATH="$HOME/.local/zig:$PATH"
zig build quick-start
```

The program starts a listener on a free local port, publishes a greeter,
connects a second peer, and checks its `Hello, world` reply. A line beginning
`01: greeter fetched by name` confirms success.

Read [the complete program](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig)
alongside the walkthrough below. [Examples]($link.page('examples')) continues
with introductions, restricted access, and state that survives a restart.

# [In Zig]($section.id('in-zig'))

glowcap is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package. Use the compiler version in
the [toolchain pins](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/dependencies.md),
then add a pinned checkout to your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap#COMMIT_SHA
```

Replace `COMMIT_SHA` with the revision you want to pin. The public API can
still change, so move the pin deliberately.

```zig
const glowcap = b.dependency("glowcap", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("glowcap", glowcap.module("glowcap"));
```

The `glowcap` module and the Noise TCP example are pure Zig.
[Other transports]($link.ref('add-other-transports')) need additional imports.

# Dial

Each tab sets up one transport, and the code after the tabs is the same for
all of them. Start with **noise-tcp**, TCP sealed with the
[Noise]($link.page('transports/noise')) protocol, between two peers that can
already reach each other. For a deployment, pick a row from
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) and use its tab.
Plain `tcp` is cleartext and for local tests only.

One iroh endpoint carries many connections, so the iroh tab registers it once and
every connection shares it. Everything after that works as it does on the other
transports.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:zig GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **noise-tcp**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const glowcap = @import("glowcap");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !void {
>     // Same TCP sockets as cleartext keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
>     // The designator is the peer's durable key; the hint is where to dial.
>     var hint_storage: [1]glowcap.ocapn.model.HintEntry = undefined;
>     const peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
>     const partner = glowcap.net.tcp.noise.locator(
>         peer_key,
>         "127.0.0.1:9099",
>         &hint_storage,
>     );
>
>     const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
>         allocator,
>         io,
>         .{ .symbols = &.{.noise_tcp} },
>         partner,
>         .ephemeral,
>         .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
>     );
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const glowcap = @import("glowcap");
> const iroh = @import("iroh");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator, partner_uri: []const u8) !void {
>     const partner = try glowcap.ocapn.model.uri.parsePeer(allocator, partner_uri);
>     defer glowcap.ocapn.model.freePeerLocator(allocator, partner);
>     var endpoint = try iroh.Resident.init(io, null, .{ .reachability = .discovery });
>     defer endpoint.deinit() catch |err| std.log.err("iroh endpoint: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
>     var adapter = try iroh.MultiplexedAdapter.init(allocator, &endpoint);
>     defer if (adapter.deinit()) |failure| std.log.err("iroh adapter: {s}", .{failure.primaryName()});
>     const residents = [_]glowcap.driver.HostRuntime.ProviderRegistration{
>         .{ .profile = iroh.transport_profile, .resident = adapter.multiplexed() },
>     };
>
>     const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
>         allocator,
>         io,
>         .{ .residents = &residents },
>         partner,
>         .ephemeral,
>         .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
>     );
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const glowcap = @import("glowcap");
> const webrtc = @import("webrtc");
>
> // `signaling` carries the offer and each candidate to the peer the locator
> // names, over a path you already hold, such as an OCapN capability both peers
> // can reach. The answer comes back through `Backend.provideAnswer`.
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator, partner_uri: []const u8, signaling: webrtc.SignalingAuthority) !void {
>     const partner = try glowcap.ocapn.model.uri.parsePeer(allocator, partner_uri);
>     defer glowcap.ocapn.model.freePeerLocator(allocator, partner);
>     var endpoint = try webrtc.Resident.init(.{});
>     defer endpoint.deinit() catch |err| std.log.err("webrtc endpoint: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
>     var resident = try webrtc.CompletionResident.init(
>         allocator,
>         try webrtc.Backend.initBound(allocator, &endpoint, signaling, 256, 16 * 1024),
>         .{},
>     );
>     defer resident.deinit();
>     const residents = [_]glowcap.driver.HostRuntime.ProviderRegistration{
>         .{ .profile = webrtc.transport_profile, .resident = resident.resident() },
>     };
>
>     const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
>         allocator,
>         io,
>         .{ .residents = &residents },
>         partner,
>         .ephemeral,
>         .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
>     );
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const glowcap = @import("glowcap");
> const relay_tls = @import("relay_tls");
>
> pub fn example(io: std.Io, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !void {
>     // Noise_XK seals the session end to end; the relay forwards ciphertext.
>     const relay_url = "wss://relay.example/relay";
>     var tls = switch (relay_tls.initTlsOwner(io, allocator, .system)) {
>         .ready => |ready| ready.owner,
>         .failed => return error.RelayUnavailable,
>     };
>     defer switch (tls.release()) {
>         .released => {},
>         .busy, .failed => std.log.err("relay TLS teardown failed", .{}),
>     };
>     var entropy: [std.Random.DefaultCsprng.secret_seed_length]u8 = undefined;
>     try io.randomSecure(&entropy);
>     const transports: glowcap.driver.Transports = .{ .symbols = &.{.noise_relay}, .relay = .{
>         .url = relay_url,
>         .entropy_seed = entropy,
>         .resolver_io = io,
>         .tls_owner = tls,
>     } };
>     var hint_storage: [1]glowcap.ocapn.model.HintEntry = undefined;
>     const peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>";
>     const partner = glowcap.net.relay.noise.locator(peer_key, relay_url, &hint_storage);
>     const session = try glowcap.driver.connect(
>         allocator,
>         io,
>         transports,
>         partner,
>         .ephemeral,
>         .{ .handshake = .{ .ms = 10_000 }, .settle = .{ .ms = 10_000 } },
>     );
> ```
